      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The city boasts 13 named lakes, including Arbor, Cedar Island, and Pike, making it a popular spot for water‑based recreation.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maple_Grove%2C_Minnesota",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Pierre Bottineau House, built in 1854, stands as the first wood‑frame home in the township and now resides in Elm Creek Park Reserve.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18,
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maple_Grove%2C_Minnesota",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Maple Grove’s abundant maple trees once made it a significant source of maple syrup, a tradition that helped shape the town’s early economy.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maple_Grove%2C_Minnesota",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Minnesota’s largest Hindu temple is located in Maple Grove, serving as a cultural hub for the state’s Hindu community.",
